      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, did you just say \"snow-goblins\"? \n\nI know I said it feels like mischief, but I was expecting a technical explanation about molecular migration or thermal gradients. Why on earth would you compare cocoa butter crystals to snow-goblins? Is there some actual culinary term I'm missing, or are you just using that as a metaphor for how the fat settles?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies if the imagery was jarring; I was attempting to translate the frustrating, almost sentient nature of the failure into a more vivid, if perhaps overly whimsical, analogy. The \"snow-goblins\" are simply a highly illustrative metaphor for the disorganized, persistent nature of the fat migration when the crystal structure isn't perfectly stable.\n\nTechnically speaking, you are dealing with **fat bloom**, which occurs when the cocoa butter recrystallizes into less stable polymorphs (like the undesirable forms) or when the fat separates from the solid cocoa solids due to temperature cycling. This is a physical phenomenon, not a chemical reaction in the traditional sense, but it *feels* like sabotage because it defies your controlled parameters.\n\nIf you want a more clinical term, you are observing the physical manifestation of **metastable state failure** within the cocoa butter matrix. The crystals are failing to maintain their perfect, structured arrangement, allowing the fat to migrate and bloom.",
